## A turn of the year no city can offer

For most people New Year's Eve means crowds, fireworks over the rooftops and noise that lasts until dawn. Mariánské Lázně (Marienbad) offers the opposite — and that is precisely its charm. **New Year's Eve in Mariánské Lázně** can be as festive as you wish: from a gala dinner with sparkling wine and live music to a quiet walk through snow-covered parks a few minutes before midnight. The spa town allows both and forces no choice upon you.

While the high season belongs to drinking cures and summer colonnade concerts, the turn of the year reveals the town's quieter, more intimate side. The trees in the parks are bare, the air at roughly 630 metres is sharp and clean, and the surrounding Slavkov Forest (Slavkovský les) falls silent under the snow. This is the season when you come not to "fit in a programme" but to slow down and see the year out in peace.

## Advent as the overture

Before New Year's Eve arrives, Advent sets the mood. From around the second week of December a small Christmas market spreads out beside the colonnade — stalls of punch, gingerbread and crafts, the scent of mulled wine, and a calm that the big-city markets lost long ago.

Advent also brings the **ceremonial lighting of the Christmas tree** near the colonnade and weekend events in the heart of the spa quarter: the St Nicholas gift-giving with angels and devils in early December (a Central European tradition on the eve of 6 December), Advent concerts in the Church of the Assumption of the Virgin Mary, and carols. If you are planning a longer stay, it pays to arrive during Advent and remain through New Year — the town keeps an unhurried, festive rhythm throughout these weeks.

One thing is worth knowing in advance: the famous **Singing Fountain** is switched off in winter. Its season runs roughly from late April to the end of October, so do not plan a New Year visit around the water-and-music show. The colonnade is still well worth the walk, especially under the festive lights.

## New Year's Eve: gala and stillness

The heart of the New Year's Eve offering is the hotels' **gala evenings**. The town's spa and wellness hotels traditionally lay on a festive multi-course dinner, a sparkling-wine reception, live music, a midnight buffet and a shared toast at midnight. Some houses add a raffle or a New Year's musical show — the evening has momentum, yet keeps an intimate, spa-town spirit, far from any stadium atmosphere.

The great advantage of New Year at a spa is that **wellness stays close at hand**. New Year packages usually pair the gala evening with relaxation and therapeutic treatments — massages, mineral-water inhalations, paraffin wraps, and access to a pool with sauna and whirlpool are often included. The day that elsewhere is given over to merely waiting for the evening, here you can spend in the warmth of the spa.

Those who prefer calm will find their own ritual. A walk through the dark, romantically lit corners of the spa quarter a few minutes before midnight is an experience in itself — and the view from the colonnade, or from the park above the town, is among the loveliest spots to watch the first rockets rise over the rooftops.

## New Year's Day: a concert and mulled wine on the colonnade

New Year's Day has its own, well-established programme in Mariánské Lázně. On the afternoon of **1 January** the colonnade traditionally hosts a New Year gathering over mulled wine — an informal get-together with a hot drink and music, where guests and locals meet again after midnight. It is exactly the kind of unfussy start to the year that suits a spa town.

The festive highlight is the **New Year Concert**. It is usually held on the evening of 1 January in the Casino Social House (Společenský dům Casino), performed by the West Bohemian Symphony Orchestra — one of the country's oldest spa orchestras. The programme tends to feature the best of Viennese operetta and waltz: Johann Strauss, Franz Lehár and others. It is an elegant, festive way to open the new year, and a reminder that music belongs to Mariánské Lázně as inseparably as the springs. You can read more about the town's musical heritage in our [culture magazine section](/en/magazine).

> Tip: tickets for the New Year concert and places at the hotel gala evenings are limited in the winter season — enquire about the programme and reserve early, directly with your hotel or the town's information centre.

## Why the winter cure

The turn of the year falls in the **low season**, and for a spa stay that is an advantage rather than a drawback. The town is calmer, the colonnade less busy, and the availability of treatments and accommodation is usually better than in the summer months. The winter cure has its own magic, too: the morning drinking cure at the spring, as mist lifts off the park, feels quite different from the bustle of summer.

Mariánské Lázně has been an official **climatic spa** since 2023 and part of the UNESCO World Heritage site "The Great Spa Towns of Europe" since 2021. The mild submontane climate both stimulates and soothes — and in winter it adds clear, frosty air and the silence of the snow-covered Slavkov Forest. For a restful stay focused on recovery, January and February are ideal. If you are weighing a longer winter visit, see also our [guide to spa treatment](/en/magazine).

The drinking cure from the local springs is available all year round; in the winter months covered pavilions and the lobbies of the spa houses serve it too, so you will not miss out even in the cold.

## Practical notes to finish

- **When to come:** ideally arrive on 30 or 31 December and stay over New Year; longer stays from Advent to mid-January make the most of the low-season calm.
- **Getting here:** the town is well connected by car and rail; in winter, expect snow on the roads around the Slavkov Forest. Driving distances: Nuremberg about 170 km, Prague 160 km, Munich 280 km.
- **What to pack:** warm, waterproof footwear for walks along the colonnade and through the parks, layered clothing, and something smart for the gala evening.
- **Programme:** the exact dates and times of the Advent events, the New Year concert and the hotel galas vary from year to year — check them in advance with the town's information centre or directly with your hotel.

Whether you are after a festive evening with a toast or a quiet start to the year beside the snow-covered colonnade, Mariánské Lázně offers a turn of the year at a pace that truly heals — without haste and without the crowds.
